aloha-oe -- a musical toy

aloha-oe plays Aloha 'Oe, with synthesized instruments and an optional
singing voice.  It can also write the result to a stereo WAV file.  The
score and instrument definitions are compiled into the program; there
are no data files to install.

The arrangement takes its key, pace and principal instrumentation from
Johnny Cash's recording on American VI: Ain't No Grave.  The melody was
checked against early printed editions and an independent transcription.
The instruments use additive synthesis.  The voice uses a glottal source
and moving formants, with a small phoneme interpreter for articulation.
All sound is calculated by the program.  No recorded samples are used.

BUILDING

You need a C11 compiler, make, and the system development headers.  The
command-line parser uses getopt_long().  Audio output uses native system
interfaces; no separate audio library is required.

        make
        make check
        ./aloha-oe

See INSTALL for compiler options, installation and cross-compilation.
The checks do not open an audio device.

USING IT

The default is an instrumental performance in E major, lasting about
three minutes.  To add the voice, or save a performance:

        ./aloha-oe -e
        ./aloha-oe -e -s chorus --seconds 24
        ./aloha-oe -ne -o aloha.wav
        ./aloha-oe -ne --voice-style retro -o retro.wav

The -e option plays the voice and melody instrument together.  Use -V
for voice with accompaniment, or -m instrumental for instruments alone.
The voice is deliberately simple; some words are only just intelligible.

Tempo is measured in printed quarter notes.  The default is 49 beats
per minute, with an eighth-note accompaniment at 98.  Setting -b 98
doubles the speed.  The -v option sets the peak level of the generated
signal; it does not change the system mixer.

Words are printed as they are sung in vocal and ensemble modes.  Use
--no-lyrics to suppress them, or -l to show them during an instrumental
performance.  Lyrics go to standard output; diagnostics go to standard
error.  The -n option disables playback and lyric output.

Run ./aloha-oe -h for a list of options.  The aloha-oe(6) manual describes
individual parts, score export, phoneme input and device selection:

        man ./doc/aloha-oe.6

PORTABILITY

Audio backends are supplied for Darwin, FreeBSD, DragonFly BSD, Linux,
Windows, NetBSD and OpenBSD.  The Darwin backend has been built and run
on macOS (arm64).  The other backends have not yet been tested on their
target systems.  See doc/PORTING for details and remaining restrictions.

Linux and OpenBSD use audio device nodes directly.  A sound server may
already have the device open.  Choose a suitable device with -d, or use
-n -o to write a WAV file.  Unsupported systems can still render files.

The program renders the whole selection before starting playback.  A
full performance at 48000 Hz needs about 69 MB for its sample buffer.

THE SOURCE TREE

        src/            score, synthesis, command line and file output
        src/audio/      native PCM playback backends
        doc/            manual, implementation notes and references

The source distribution contains no executable or example recordings.
Generated files are not build inputs.  Run make check for the built-in
score and synthesis checks.

doc/NOTES explains the arrangement and synthesis.  doc/REFERENCES lists
the score editions, recording notes and algorithm references.  COPYING
contains the MIT license and the composition credit.

Bug reports should include the operating system, compiler, command line
and complete diagnostic.  For an audio problem, include the device name
and whether writing a WAV file succeeds.
